---
import Root from '#/layout/root.astro'
import { Image } from 'astro:assets'
import vibeCheck from '#/assets/vibe-check.png'
import vibeCheckModeration from '#/assets/vibe-check-moderation.png'
import vibeCheckGame from '#/assets/vibe-check-gameview.png'
import vibeCheckTarget from '#/assets/vibe-check-target.png'
import vibeCheckSuggestion from '#/assets/vibe-check-suggestion.png'
---

<Root
  title="Case Study: Vibe Check!"
  description="Case study of the creation of Vibe Check! a web-based multiplayer game for Twitch streamers and their chat."
>
  <article class="prose flow">
    <h1>Case Study: Vibe Check!</h1>
    <p class="text-lg">
      <a href="https://vibecheck.mxbranch.com" target="_blank">Vibe Check!</a> is
      a web-based multiplayer game designed for Twitch streamers to play with their
      chat, made in collaboration with streamer Jack at <a
        href="https://jackdaw.tv"
        target="_blank"
      >
        Jackdaw.tv
      </a>.
    </p>
    <figure class="prose__fullwidth flow">
      <Image
        src={vibeCheck}
        alt="Vibe Check! Welcome Screen"
        loading="eager"
        width={800}
        quality="high"
        layout="constrained"
      />
      <figcaption>The initial welcome screen of the game.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <section class="prose__fullwidth with-sidebar">
      <div class="sidebar">
        <h2>Requirements</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="flow">
        <p>
          Jack was interested in creating a version of the game <a
            href="https://www.cmyk.games/collections/games/products/wavelength"
            target="_blank"
          >
            Wavelength by CMYK Games
          </a> which they could play with their Twitch stream audience. It needed:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Stream safety features such as moderation</li>
          <li>Mobile device accessibility</li>
          <li>A polished user interface</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="with-sidebar prose__fullwidth">
      <div class="sidebar">
        <h2>Tools and Libraries</h2>
      </div>
      <div>
        <ul role="list">
          <li>React</li>
          <li>Tanstack Start / Query / Form</li>
          <li>Drizzle + Postgres</li>
          <li>Better-Auth</li>
          <li>Tailwind</li>
          <li>Valibot</li>
          <li>TypeScript</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
    <h2>Ideation</h2>
    <p>
      Wavelength is a game where teams compete to see who can most accurately
      guess a target on a scale based on their team member's prompt. (For an
      example of play, I recommend <a
        href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pS-XT-5R26Q"
        target="_blank"
      >
        Polygon's gameplay video
      </a>. NB: this video contains coarse language.) In adapting the idea for
      this project, I wanted to encourage a more collaborative approach to the
      game. It's not streamer vs chat, it's streamer <em>with</em> chat. To encourage
      this collaborative atmosphere:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        I decided not to include scoring in the game. If the aim of the game
        were "the streamer wants to get a good score", this could encourage
        unsportsmanlike behaviour from chat players trying to sabotage that
        score. Instead, the challenge of the game becomes, "Can I get the
        streamer to guess correctly?"
      </li>
      <li>
        Since this game is intended for live chat audiences, I wanted the scales
        to encourage discussion and engagement. My friends and I generated a
        number of scales ranging from more straightforward ("hot to cold", "tall
        to short") to more subjective ("mountain to molehill", "gigantic anime
        sword to butter knife").
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Tech Choices</h2>
    <p>
      This game required UI that responds in real time to the game state, so I
      elected to make a React <abbr title="Single Page Application">SPA</abbr> with
      an API for server-side data fetching and mutations. This does limit people who
      choose not to load JavaScript, <a
        href="https://piccalil.li/blog/a-handful-of-reasons-javascript-wont-be-available/"
        target="_blank"
      >
        or for whom JavaScript doesn't load for whatever reason
      </a>. For this project, a plurality of visitors to the site will be coming
      from Twitch, a platform which straight up doesn't load anything but a
      skeleton if JavaScript is disabled. In this scenario, I think it's
      reasonable to assume a visitor will have JavaScript on, and if other
      issues arise they can be dealt with on a case-by-case basis.
    </p>

    <p>
      I chose Tanstack Start as a framework since it provides everything I
      needed: routing capabilities, being able to define type-safe <abbr
        title="Remote Procedure Calls"
      >
        RPCs
      </abbr> for data manipulation on the server, and compatibility with the other
      libraries I'd chosen for the project. It was also an excuse to give it a go,
      which is something I'd been trying to find for a little while!
    </p>

    <p>
      Other libraries were chosen largely through pragmatism: this project
      needed a fairly quick turnaround, so I wanted to use tools I was familiar
      with and knew could get the job done.
    </p>

    <h2>The User Interface</h2>

    <p>
      The UI needed to meet two distinct requirements: being accessible on a
      wide variety of devices, and being a fun thing to look at for at least an
      hour of streaming time! Once I had the business logic of the game sorted,
      I spent time making mobile-first user interfaces for each route. I tried
      to <a href="https://buildexcellentwebsit.es/" target="_blank">
        avoid using breakpoints and media queries for this where possible
      </a>, instead leaning on flexbox and grid to do most of the work. Where
      media queries were used, they were for adjusting text size, padding, or
      occasionally flex direction.
    </p>

    <figure
      class="prose__fullwidth flow"
      style="--figure-img-max-height: 40rem;"
    >
      <Image
        src={vibeCheckSuggestion}
        alt="Vibe Check! Player Prompt View on Mobile"
        loading="lazy"
        layout="constrained"
        width={800}
        quality="high"
      />
      <figcaption>
        The player 'prompting' view on a small smartphone viewport. The
        streamer's avatar is positioned over a target position, and the player
        has to make a prompt which the streamer can use to guess that position
        on the scale. The mid/quartile markers were added in after some user
        feedback rounds before the game was played on stream.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Once I had the overall layout for each route sorted, I then added a number
      of small design features and interactions with the aim of giving the game
      a whimsical feel. This included a slowly rotating radial 'spotlight'
      effect on the background, and a Minecraft-esque randomised subtitle on the
      home page referencing a number of in-jokes from the stream community. I
      also made the buttons respond with a little motion when hovered over.
    </p>

    <figure class="prose__fullwidth flow" style="--figure-img-y: 68%;">
      <!-- Don't pay attention to the magic number behind the curtain -->
      <Image
        src={vibeCheckGame}
        alt="Vibe Check! Game View"
        loading="lazy"
        layout="constrained"
        width={800}
        quality="high"
      />
      <figcaption>
        The main interface for the streamer to guess based on user prompts. The
        slider interface is prominent on the screen and shows the streamer's
        avatar as the handle. The chat player's prompt is shown as a speech
        bubble coming from their avatar, giving players a little moment in the
        spotlight.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="prose__fullwidth flow" style="--figure-img-y: 64%;">
      <!-- Don't pay attention to the magic number behind the curtain -->
      <Image
        src={vibeCheckTarget}
        alt="Vibe Check! Game View showing the prompt target"
        loading="lazy"
        layout="constrained"
        width={800}
        quality="high"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Once a guess is locked in, the target fades into view. It's shown as a
        series of concentric circles, giving immediate visual feedback about the
        accuracy of the streamer's guess. As you can see here, I guessed...
        poorly. <!-- The vibes were rancid. -->
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Stream Safety</h2>

    <p>
      Twitch is a platform with fairly rigid <abbr title="Terms of Service">
        ToS
      </abbr>, and any public stream is a potential target for trolls. Any time
      you're getting user input there's risks you need to account for, and in
      this case one of the risks is "anything a malicious individual types in
      might then be displayed to several dozen people and risk the streamer
      punishment from the platform". Tooling to minimize risk and ensure stream
      safety was paramount for this project. There are multiple systems in
      place:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        A player is required to sign in with an active Twitch account via OAuth.
        This doesn't fully reduce the risk of trolling, but it introduces
        friction to participation and reduces the likelihood a bot or rogue
        agent will join a game. This also let me implement a feature to let the
        streamer block accounts making unsavoury prompts.
      </li>
      <li>
        A 'moderation preview window' which could be kept on a second monitor.
        This window shows the <em>next prompt</em> that will be shown, along with
        options to skip that prompt or block the user. This allows the streamer to
        vet content before it is shown to stream, and enact moderation quickly without
        need for a separate moderator.
      </li>
      <li>
        Each game instance uses a 'room code' system for access, inspired by the
        online party games of <a
          href="https://www.jackboxgames.com/"
          target="_blank"
        >
          Jackbox Games
        </a>. This means that players need to be watching a given stream in
        order to participate in the game, and further reduces the likelihood of
        an automated intruder.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <figure class="prose__fullwidth flow" style="--figure-img-y: 53%;">
      <!-- Don't pay attention to the magic number behind the curtain -->
      <Image
        src={vibeCheckModeration}
        alt="Vibe Check! Moderation View"
        loading="lazy"
        layout="constrained"
        width={800}
        quality="high"
      />
      <figcaption>
        The moderation view allows the streamer to check the next prompt and
        engage in moderation actions.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Outcomes</h2>

    <p>
      After a few rounds of testing the system with my friends (and fixing the
      bugs and interface issues said testing surfaced), it was time for Jack to
      play the game with their stream audience. The stream was a resounding
      success (largely measured in terms of number of in-jokes created and
      number of good-natured <s>arguments</s> discussions spawned in the chat!).
    </p>

    <p>
      Jack has brought the game back for multiple streams since its premiere,
      based on audience request.
    </p>

    <figure class="prose__fullwidth">
      <iframe
        src="/cookie-gate/vibe-check-twitch/"
        allowfullscreen="true"
        height="378"
        width="620"
        title="Twitch video clip embed"
      >
      </iframe>
      <figcaption>
        The vibes were good! NB: the full stream contains coarse language and
        adult topics, view at your discretion.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </article>
</Root>

